<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martti Ahtisaari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/2023">2023</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martti Ahtisaari (1937–2023) was a Finnish diplomat and Social Democratic politician who served as president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> from 1994 to 2000. As a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> official he led the transition to Namibian independence in 1989–1990, and he later mediated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> in 1999, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> in 2005 and in the talks on Kosovo's status. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 2008 for his work resolving international conflicts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martti-ahtisaari.jpg" alt="Martti Ahtisaari" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Markku Niskanen · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martti_Ahtisaari_M%C3%A4ntyniemess%C3%A4_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Martti Ahtisaari was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a> (now Vyborg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>) on 23 June 1937. His father, Oiva, a non-commissioned officer in the supply troops, was at the front during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continuation-war">Continuation War</a>, and his mother, Tyyne, took him to Kuopio in 1940, where he spent most of his childhood. The family moved to Oulu in 1952, and he graduated from high school in 1956 and qualified as a primary-school teacher in 1959. In 1960 he became director of a physical education boarding school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. In 1965 he joined the Ministry for Foreign Affairs, where he helped set up its International Development Assistance Office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His diplomatic career began in 1973 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>'s ambassador to Tanzania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. In 1977 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> recalled him to succeed Seán MacBride as Commissioner for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>. In April 1989 he went to Namibia as UN Special Representative to head the United Nations Transition Assistance Group. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> fighters crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, he approved the deployment of South African troops to restore order, and in the fighting that followed at least 375 SWAPO insurgents were killed; some observers thought he had made too many concessions to the South Africans. After the independence elections he and his wife were made honorary Namibian citizens in 1992.</p><p>He was a UN under-secretary-general from 1987 to 1991, Secretary of State at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>'s Ministry for Foreign Affairs from 1991, and in 1992-1993 chaired the working group on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> at the Conference on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. Elected in the first direct presidential election, he beat Elisabeth Rehn in the second round and was sworn in on 1 March 1994. He supported Finland's entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, approved by 57 per cent in a 1994 referendum, and in 1999 negotiated with Viktor Chernomyrdin to end the fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a>. He did not stand for re-election, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarja-halonen">Tarja Halonen</a> succeeded him on 1 March 2000. He was the last strong president before the 2000 constitution reduced the office's powers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He became chairman of the International Crisis Group in 2000 and founded the Crisis Management Initiative. In November 2005 UN Secretary-General Kofi Annan appointed him Special Envoy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> status process, which ended in 2007 and was followed by Kosovo's declaration of independence in February 2008. On 10 October 2008 he was announced as winner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> for efforts to resolve international conflicts over more than three decades. He died in Helsinki on 16 October 2023, aged 86, after an illness announced in 2021, and was buried at Hietaniemi Cemetery after a state funeral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Martti Ahtisaari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Finnish diplomats and soldiers took part in United Nations peacekeeping, and former president <b>Martti Ahtisaari</b> received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2008 for his mediation in Namibia, Aceh and Kosovo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Vyborg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Helsinki, in what is now <a href="/countries/finland">Finland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=60.71,28.75&amp;year=1992" aria-label="Vyborg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5147 136 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Martti Ahtisaari"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5597 406h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5597 406h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=60.71,28.75&amp;year=1992">What was here in 1992? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
